WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:08.710 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  a lot of art from the middle ages is so  bizarre it seems beyond explanation  which is why it's no surprise that these  seemingly nonsensical images have become  popular sources of memes in recent years  it's hard not to look at these visuals  and ask yourself wait what is going on  here so for this entry into the archive  i'll try to track down the real meaning  behind some of these images which have  circulated on the internet without  context but keep in mind that what i've  found in my research is that for many  illustrations medieval art historians  are just as clueless as the rest of us  with that said let's begin to start we  have the curious trend of medieval  babies who look a little off the sheer  amount of unfortunate looking babies in  medieval and later renaissance art  suggests more is going on than just a  few misplaced brushstrokes portraits of  medieval infants seem like they were  intentionally styled to look like tiny  middle-aged men and indeed that is more  or less what's going on here these  artists are following the trend of the  homunculus which translates to little  man in latin the idea behind the  homunculus is related to the baby jesus  who was the most popular infant to paint  at the time due to a very literal  interpretation of the church's idea that  jesus was born fully formed and  unchanged a lot of classic religious art  from the era has a very adult face to  jesus and paintings of other infants  started to follow this trend as well  hence babies being painted with receding  hairlines and layers of muscle by the  time renaissance styles developed over  more of europe however the somewhat  unsettling trend of adult-faced infants  went out of practice and frescoes  thankfully started featuring much more  normal looking babies  switching topics another unusual theme  in medieval art that the internet has  picked up on is that in scenes of people  meeting their untimely demise which  should be pretty grim the sufferers seem  strangely laid back seriously all of  these people seem pretty chill about  their imminent death all things  considered in fact a lot of them appear  somewhat bored or at most subtly annoyed  by the situation which is an unusual  reaction to such a serious event so why  are there entire battle scenes where  everyone seems completely checked out  well one explanation is that the neutral  expressions come from the cultural value  of greeting death with a calmness so  you'll be rewarded in what comes after  while staying calm and grounded can be  healthy i will say i think some of these  people might be taking their chillness a  little too far  and while we're on the subject of  strange portrayals of death death itself  as personified by skeletal figures  coming to haunt the living often looked  a little goofier than one might expect  which has led to a variety of jokes  online most of these illustrations are  actually related to the concept of the  dance macabre or dance of death the  dance macabre was a concept that death  was an equalizer coming for all classes  of feudal society the images were  designed to remind people to see this  life's fleeting nature and to embrace  death when the time came a related and  also strangely amusing image that has  become popular online is this portrait  of three individuals having a bizarre  reaction to a group of skeletons the  image is actually based on a legend  called the three living and the three  dead in which three nobles encounter  three skeletons who are actually undead  reflections of the nobles sent to  frighten them into changing their  immoral ways it's a pretty unique tale  with a lot of variations and one which  shows up a fair amount to medieval art  moving on one particularly strange trend  in medieval art that has captured some  attention online is how some artists  depicted rabbits various manuscripts  portray rabbits not as we know them as  small fuzzy animals that aren't much of  a threat to a human but as large  threatening creatures carrying off  helpless villagers it's a concept that  certainly stands out in the margins of  illuminated texts so what is going on  here are these some kind of mythical  beasts did rabbits have some kind of  attitude problem in the middle ages well  the most accepted theory is that these  strange hilarious images are meant to be  just that jokes just like how we share  funny images today this sort of bizarre  art was probably something medieval  monks had to laugh about with their  friends and yes this trend is somewhat  similar to monty python's famous deadly  rabbit's got a vicious  briefly leaving the medieval era for a  series of portraits i felt i had to  include we have the highly unusual 18th  century self-portraits of french painter  joseph de croix although trained in the  classical style the successful decroy  became unsatisfied with the constraints  of traditional portraiture which at the  time were almost entirely serious with  somber body language and reserved  compositions so decroy started creating  art with absurd larger than life  expressions that really go against the  standards of the time in breaking these  constraints his works actually resemble  the earlier 16th and 17th century style  of portraiture called trones which were  paintings like the one you see before  you that frequently featured exaggerated  facial expressions some of these  expressions are so unusual that it might  seem hard to believe they aren't modern  parodies but despite what most classical  portraits might lead you to believe some  people from the past had a very modern  sense of humor and decroy was certainly  one of those people today his most  famous image is probably this one where  he painted himself pointing at viewers  and laughing at them it says something  that this image is still funny to people  on the internet hundreds of years after  it was painted before we reach our final  topic there are a few other  miscellaneous drawings i'd like to try  and address first we have this amusing  illustration of what seems to be a  mythical bird monster carrying a smaller  creature and a human away on its back  and the two passengers appear so deep in  argument they haven't noticed their  being hoisted away despite appearances  this isn't a medieval illustration but a  depiction of a costumed individual at a  17th century fair with the figures on  their back likely some kind of puppets  pretty unusual next we have this image  of a man who seems like he's being  wrapped up in a sheet by his friends  despite the goofy vibe this image is  likely vitalis of milan a saint who  according to legend was sentenced to  death so those two aren't his friends  but his executioners and all likelihood  lowering him into his grave and finally  on a lighter note we've got this  famously weird portrait of a baby and no  this isn't one of those bizarre medieval  era portraits but a stylized portrait by  the 20th and 21st century figurative  artist fernando botero whose unique  style sometimes causes people to assume  his work was painted centuries prior  and closing out this entry into the  archive we have a topic i've touched on  in the past but is always worth  revisiting that being the bizarre  medieval trend of depicting knight's  fighting snails you can find these  bizarre showdowns hidden within the  margins of many manuscripts there's a  strangeness here that is pretty apparent  i mean snails don't seem like the most  obvious creatures to depict as  gargantuan menacing juggernauts and  maybe i'm over analyzing but to me  there's a sense in these images that the  knights might not make it these snails  seem like they mean business and this  wasn't a one-off concept we have over 65  documented images of snails doing battle  with various warriors and what's most  intriguing is that we're not totally  sure why with theories ranging from the  snails representing class divisions to  being a joke about the concept of a  knight's armor to a metaphoric  personification of the slow and steady  approach of death or maybe this is just  another example of medieval artists  trying to create something to make their  friends laugh just like what we do  online today  and that's all the weird medieval art we  have time for in this century i made  other videos covering unusual medieval  depictions of animals and mythical  creatures that explain additional  bizarre illustrations i didn't get to  hear thank you for watching if you  enjoyed this entry please lend your  support and like subscribe and hit the  notification icon to stay up to date on  all things curious see you in the next  video  [Music]

